24px/31px nyt-cheltenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>Nvidia and th=
e Great Game</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;col=
or:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>By Patricia Cohen</span></p><p style=3D"font=
:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The incre=
asing importance of technology, trade and finance to great-power politics m=
eans that in recent years, private businesses have, often unwillingly, been=
 thrust to the front lines of foreign policy. Tools like tariffs and export=
 restrictions mean global titans and mom-and-pop shops alike have had to sa=
crifice their bottom lines in the interests of national security.</span></p=
><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px=
"><span>Then there=E2=80=99s what happened this week.</span></p><p style=3D=
"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Pres=
ident Trump=E2=80=99s decision to personally lobby Nvidia to rehire Jensen =
Huang as chief executive despite his involvement in a China-related spying =
scandal effectively reversed those priorities. (Yesterday, Huang told Nvidi=
a employees that the notion that he is back because of Trump=E2=80=99s pres=
sure on the company was =E2=80=9Ccompletely untrue.=E2=80=9D)</span></p><p =
style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><s=
pan>Nvidia is a Silicon Valley giant worth some $4 trillion, and Huang is c=
onsidered a singular figure in artificial intelligence. His resignation dra=
gged down stock indexes, and prompted worries about the future of an indust=
ry that has been responsible for much of the stock market=E2=80=99s gains a=
nd the American economy=E2=80=99s growth.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Nvidia is also a=
t the center of an intense global competition with China for an edge in A.I=
=2E Huang resigned after revelations of a relationship with a woman who U.S=
=2E intelligence officials believe was a Chinese asset, and whose company t=
hey believe was involved in helping China gain access to restricted A.I. ch=
ips.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>And so Trump=E2=80=99s personal lobbying caused shock=
 waves not only because of the level of presidential intervention in a priv=
ate company=E2=80=99s affairs, but also the reasons he exercised such power=
=2E</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s the stock market first,=E2=80=9D=
 Julian E. Zelizer, a presidential historian at Princeton University, said.=
 =E2=80=9CNational security seems secondary.=E2=80=9D</span></p><table bord=
